    "description": "Iranian open-pit case studies show how loaders, shovels, haul trucks, and unloading points function as an interconnected production system. Their evidence highlights fleet allocation and equipment availability, while leaving important gaps in the wider picture of extraction machinery.",
    "tags": ["Mining equipment", "Open-pit mining", "Haulage", "Sungun", "Golgohar"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:19:45Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:19:45Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Extraction Equipment in Iranian Mining | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "What Sungun and Golgohar research reveals about loading, haulage, equipment availability, and the limits of available fleet evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "system",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Equipment works as a system",
        "content": "An open-pit haulage fleet links the working face to the next destination for extracted material. Loading machines fill trucks, which transport material to unloading points and return for another cycle. A study at Iran's Sungun copper mine examined this interaction through simulation-based optimization, explicitly accounting for loading, hauling, dumping, and return times. [s1]\n\nThe central problem was allocation: how to assign available trucks to loading locations while meeting production requirements. Too few trucks can leave loading equipment waiting; too many can create queues. The engineering question therefore extends beyond the size or brand of an individual machine to the coordination of the whole operation. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "sungun",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Sungun: matching loading and transport",
        "content": "The Sungun research documented a mixed loading system involving an excavator, loaders, and a shovel. Its case-study layout distinguished ore destinations from a waste dump, an important distinction because material movement includes both valuable feed and material removed to maintain access. These were inputs describing the studied operation, not a verified inventory for the editorial cutoff. [s1]\n\nThe authors modeled variable operating times and evaluated bottlenecks through waiting, utilization, and transported material. Their proposed allocations were simulation results under specified constraints. They should be read as an analysis of possible operating arrangements rather than evidence that every recommendation was implemented. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "availability",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Golgohar: a fleet must also be available",
        "content": "Research published in 2026 used reliability information from Golgohar Iron Ore Mine No. 1 to construct an agent-based haulage simulation. In this kind of model, trucks, shovels, and loading or dumping points are represented as interacting units whose states change as operations proceed. The study considered congestion, equipment readiness, changing mine geometry, and failures. [s2]\n\nIts reported scenarios identified truck availability as a major constraint and examined coordinated improvements in truck and shovel availability. These are modeled outcomes, not independently verified gains from a completed fleet-renewal program. The distinction matters: a simulation can identify a promising intervention without demonstrating that its assumptions can be achieved in everyday operation. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What these cases establish",
        "content": "Together, Sungun and Golgohar illustrate why extraction capacity cannot be understood from machine counts alone. Assignment, operating delays, and readiness affect how installed equipment contributes to material movement. The studies concern particular mine systems, however, and do not establish the condition of Iran's national mining fleet. [s1][s2]\n\nA complete account of extraction equipment also needs verified coverage of drilling and rock-breaking machinery, underground systems, and the supply and maintenance arrangements supporting them. Those subjects remain outside the evidence established here. This page therefore offers a supported introduction to open-pit loading and haulage while retaining a research-needed status for its broader equipment scope.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "haulage-cycle",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "A simplified loading and haulage cycle",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Loading point\"] -->|\"Loaded truck travels\"| B[\"Destination selection\"]\n  B -->|\"Ore delivery\"| C[\"Ore crusher\"]\n  B -->|\"Waste delivery\"| D[\"Waste dump\"]\n  C -->|\"Empty truck returns\"| E[\"Return journey\"]\n  D -->|\"Empty truck returns\"| E\n  E -->|\"Next loading cycle\"| A\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows represent material transport and truck circulation in the Sungun case-study layout, not a complete extraction process or a current mine plan.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      }
    ],
